@vsm 1.2.1
@status active

// policy_atomization.spec.vsm
// Author: rmw3 (S5) + Claude (S3-S5 role, this session on Sonnet 5), 2026-07-26.
// Design session, not implementation — bbh-lab owns the build (same ownership as
// program_intel_canonical.spec.vsm). Extends that spec's capture pipeline + the
// LM-as-function/verify-loop architecture in memory project_bbh_mining_strategy.
// Triggered by empirical evidence: bbh-lab's Stage 1-3 corpus work (15 programs,
// commit range around 2026-07-26) found heading-based section tagging fragile —
// PayPal and Coinbase put everything into one "Overview" blob with no sub-structure,
// Airbnb has a 2-level structure with an embedded subprogram (HotelTonight). This
// is not a parsing bug to fix better; for some programs the categorization task is
// genuinely a comprehension problem on free prose, not a structural lookup.

spec(POLICY-ATOMIZATION) {

  premise: "Across the 15-program corpus, policy text splits into (at least) two
      structural regimes: clean-sectioned (Stripe: 'Prohibited Activities' as its
      own heading — the existing bbh_guidelines_fetcher.py pattern-match handles
      this) and everything-in-Overview (PayPal, Coinbase — no heading signal at
      all, the rule is buried in prose). No amount of better regex solves the
      second regime; it requires reading and understanding the prose, which is
      inherently interpretive, not a lookup. This spec is the pipeline for that
      regime, plus the reconciliation/confidence machinery that makes it safe to
      trust at less-than-certain confidence.",

  // ── STEP 1 — split extraction by NATIVE structure, before any semantics ──────
  step_1_split_by_nature: {
    policy_scopes: "The H1 scope table (Asset name, Type, Coverage, Max severity,
        Bounty, Last update, Resolved Reports) is ALREADY structured — it comes
        from H1's own API/DOM table, not free prose. Extract it deterministically,
        no LM, no atomization. This maps onto the scope_assets/scope_assets_count/
        scope_in_scope_count/scope_oos_count fields bbh_guidelines_fetcher.py's v4
        schema already has for Stripe — formalize that as its own explicit path,
        not folded into the same function that parses prose.",
    program_guidelines: "Rewards summary, Scope exclusions, Platform standards
        deviations, and the Overview block (which itself may contain Rewards,
        Duplicates, Rules of Engagement, Testing/PoC guidance, scope notes, SLA —
        all mixed together with no reliable heading boundary). This is prose.
        Everything below (steps 2+) applies ONLY to this half — the policy_scopes
        table does not need atomization, it is already the canonical fact.",
  },

  // ── STEP 2 — atomize the prose (replaces heading-based section tagging) ─────
  step_2_atomize: {
    unit: "Per RULE OR CLAIM, not per sentence. A single compound sentence
        ('do not run automated scanners, and do not access other users'
        accounts') is TWO atoms. Atomization does not depend on document
        structure at all — this is exactly why it survives the PayPal/Coinbase
        case where section-tagging cannot: it operates on the prose directly,
        not on where a heading happens to be.",
    taxonomy: "Categories are NOT restriction-only. Every atom is potential input
        to running the bounty, not just a potential block: prohibited_method,
        pii_handling, disclosure_rule, eligibility, account_setup/enabling
        (test-account creation, required email alias, required headers — the
        account_requirements fields already in restrictions.json are exactly this
        category), out_of_scope_vuln_class, platform_standards_deviation, reward_
        and_severity_policy, and other/context (prose that states no actionable
        rule). 8 categories, not 6 — see taxonomy_v2 below for the correction.",
    taxonomy_v2: "CORRECTED 2026-07-26 after the first real corpus run (15
        programs, 328 atoms) put 180/328 (54.9%) into 'other' — too high to be
        genuine novelty, it was an incomplete taxonomy. Confirmed by sample: 'User
        role permissions without a clear and obvious security risk' (Stripe) is a
        vuln-CLASS exclusion (what does not count as a bug), a different
        dimension from prohibited_method (what technique you may not use) — this
        already has its own fields in restrictions.json (out_of_scope_
        vulnerabilities, out_of_scope_categories), it just never got its own
        atom category. 'This program has not committed to the following Platform
        Standards' (gitlab) is literally the 'Platform standards deviations'
        section named in the ORIGINAL Security Page breakdown (rmw3, this
        session) — dropped when the first taxonomy was written, restored here.
        reward_and_severity_policy (bounty/payout determination + severity
        matrices) was ~12% of all atoms on its own — common and unambiguous
        enough across the corpus to deserve its own slot rather than living in
        'other'. Two additions, not a redesign: out_of_scope_vuln_class,
        platform_standards_deviation, reward_and_severity_policy (3 new, 8
        total). Re-classify existing atoms against this — the atomization
        (span extraction) was correct, only categorization needs to re-run.",
    preprocessing_gap: "Separate from the taxonomy: some 'other' atoms were ToS
        boilerplate ('We may change the Terms at any time', 'If you don't agree
        ... you must not participate') and marketing-text remnants
        (company_description subtype, 8 atoms) that leaked through step_1's
        Path-2 prose stripping. These are not policy rules at all and should be
        filtered OUT before atomization, not atomized and then bucketed as
        other — tighten the Path-2 strip list.",
  },

  // ── STEP 3 — classify + validate PER ATOM, not per document ──────────────────
  step_3_classify_validate: {
    classify: "Per atom: which taxonomy category, is it restrictive or enabling.",
    validate: "Per atom, cite the exact source span. A diverse-verifier LM
        (distinct implementation from the classifier, per the existing
        interpretation architecture) checks EACH atom against its own citation —
        this is the thing that makes VERIFY tractable: 'does this one atom
        correctly reflect this one sentence' is a sharp, citable claim; 'does this
        whole restrictions.json correctly reflect the whole document' (the old
        unit) is fuzzy and hard to audit.",
    absence_is_corpus_relative: "The existing data_integrity_rule (prohibited_
        methods=0 with RoE present -> mark unparsed, never none) still holds at
        the document level. At atom level it sharpens: if a category is present
        in most corpus programs of similar type (e.g. every fintech program in
        the corpus has >=1 pii_handling atom) and THIS program yields zero, that
        absence is suspicious RELATIVE TO THE CORPUS, not a neutral fact. Feed it
        into the confidence score (below), don't silently accept it.",
  },

  // ── STEP 4 — reconcile against the canon (step 1's structured table) ────────
  step_4_reconcile: {
    what: "Each validated atom is checked against the policy_scopes table from
        step 1. A prose atom can AGREE, REFINE (narrow/add detail compatible with
        the table — e.g. table says asset X in-scope, prose adds 'only these vuln
        classes count for X'), or genuinely CONTRADICT (mutually exclusive claim).",
    no_hardcoded_winner: "Do NOT hardcode 'table always wins' or 'prose always
        wins' — real cases go both ways and a rigid rule will be wrong some
        fraction of the time. Also do NOT try to auto-resolve refine-vs-contradict
        at extraction time. Both cases just feed the confidence score below;
        triage looks at both sources with full context when it actually matters
        for a specific finding.",
  },

  // ── THE CONFIDENCE SCORE — the unifying resolution mechanism ────────────────
  confidence_score: {
    principle: "Per rmw3 2026-07-26: the extraction pipeline does not need to
        resolve every ambiguity with a bespoke rule. Collapse atom-vs-atom
        contradiction, atom-vs-scope discrepancy, and corpus-relative absence into
        ONE continuous signal per program (or per program+category, since a
        program can be solid on prohibited_methods and weak on pii_handling
        independently). The score does not decide anything by itself — it tells
        TRIAGE how much rigor to apply. Same attenuator logic (Ashby) as the rest
        of VSF: push the genuinely-undecidable up, don't try to pre-resolve it
        with more and more hardcoded rules.",
    inputs: [
      "internal consistency — count of atom-vs-atom contradictions found within the same program's own Guidelines text",
      "scope-table agreement — ratio of atoms that agree/refine vs contradict the policy_scopes table",
      "corpus-relative completeness — does this program have the categories similar programs typically have",
      "extraction method — atoms from clean structural sections score higher a priori than atoms extracted by atomizing free prose",
      "verifier concordance — did the diverse-verifier confirm or challenge each atom",
    ],
    consumption: "High score -> triage moves faster, trusts the structured
        extraction. Low score -> triage is explicitly told this program demands
        more rigor before a finding/action is trusted — re-read the cited prose
        directly rather than relying on the atom alone.",
    NOT_covered: "New-category discovery (an atom that fits NO existing taxonomy
        slot) is a different kind of gap — not 'low confidence in a
        classification', but 'no classification exists'. The score does not
        address this; see escalation below.",
    feeds_back_into_targeting: "Per rmw3 2026-07-26: once this score exists, it
        should not stay a triage-only signal — it belongs as an input to
        OpportunityScore's T (tractability = capability = 1/effort) in
        project_bbh_mining_strategy. T today only measures TECHNICAL effort (web/
        API vs mobile, empirical success rate); it is missing POLICY-COMPREHENSION
        effort. A program with a high raw OpportunityScore but messy/low-
        confidence policy text costs more downstream — both to run the mechanical
        gate against safely and to triage findings from it — than a
        similarly-ranked program with clean policy text. Not implemented yet
        (the score itself doesn't exist yet); recorded here so it isn't lost
        before Stage 3 lands. When it does: re-rank or re-weight candidate
        programs using the atomization confidence score, not just U/S/T from the
        coarse pass alone.",
  },

  // ── CALIBRATION — the score itself must be certified, not assumed ───────────
  calibration_gate: {
    rule: "Same pattern as Fierro Q1-Q3 (Ω convex / L<1 / σ_max) and the roadmap's
        θ_PSI=0.79 — certification is a NUMERIC THRESHOLD crossed, not a
        subjective 'looks right'. Compute the confidence score against a
        human-verified ground-truth program (Stripe, already hand-checked once)
        and confirm score >= threshold correlates with actual extraction
        correctness before trusting the score on uncalibrated programs.",
    gate: "Do not consume the confidence score in triage until this calibration
        passes. Same discipline as gate_calibracion in program_intel_canonical —
        a mechanism doesn't get trusted at scale before it reproduces a known
        truth.",
  },

  // ── NEW-CATEGORY ESCALATION — reuses the existing algedonic channel ─────────
  new_category_escalation: {
    what: "An atom that does not fit prohibited_method / pii_handling /
        disclosure_rule / eligibility / account_setup / other is NOT force-fit
        into the nearest category (that silently corrupts the taxonomy). It
        raises an algedonic signal — the SAME algedonic_ledger already audited
        this session (/root/algedonic/ledger.db on LXC 104), not a parallel
        channel. Per feedback_single_source: no new side-mechanism for this.",
    noise_handling: "Same attenuation/calibration discipline as any other
        algedonic signal (the census work already measured attenuation ~12% /
        escalation ~83% / evidence-closure ~6% for the existing signal mix). If
        new-category escalations become frequent noise, that's the signal to
        recalibrate the attenuator OR — more likely useful — notice the SAME
        shape repeating and promote it to a real taxonomy category instead of
        continuing to escalate it individually.",
  },

  // ── CONSUMPTION — two distinct operational layers ───────────────────────────
  consumption: {
    mechanical_automated: "bbh-lab (external — surface mapping) and bbh-wsl
        (internal — authenticated depth) both consume this as a DETERMINISTIC
        gate: lookup against restrictions.json + the policy_scopes table. Applies
        from the MAPPING/recon step onward, not only at submission — if a program
        excludes an asset type or forbids a recon technique, that must hold
        during discovery too, not just at the finding-validation gate.",
    triage_and_manual: "This is the PRIMARY use case for the atomized knowledge,
        not a fallback. Triage and detailed/manual exploration (bbh-wsl's
        human-assisted layer) query the validated atom list (with citations) via
        a just-in-time semantic consult for a SPECIFIC question ('is action X
        okay on program Y right now') rather than re-reading raw prose per
        question. The confidence score (above) tells this consult how much to
        trust the atom vs. how much to re-verify against the actual cited text.",
  },

  status: "DESIGN COMPLETE, not yet implemented. Builds on live artifacts: 15-
      program corpus (inbox/*_live_render.txt + CORPUS_NOTES.md), the existing
      bbh_guidelines_fetcher.py (Stripe-calibrated, to be split per step_1 rather
      than doing prose+table in one function), bbh_policy_gate.py (the
      mechanical-consumption gate, already fail-closed on unparsed).",

  next: "(1) Split bbh_guidelines_fetcher.py's cmd_from_snapshot into the two
      step_1 paths (deterministic scope-table parse vs prose capture) — smallest
      structural change, unlocks everything after it. (2) Build the atomizer
      (step_2) against the 15-program corpus already captured — no new capture
      needed. (3) Confidence score v1 with the 5 listed inputs, calibrate against
      Stripe (already hand-verified) before trusting it elsewhere. (4) Wire
      new-category atoms into the existing algedonic_ledger — do not build a new
      table or channel. (5) Only after (3) certifies: connect triage/bbh-wsl's
      manual-exploration flow to query the atom list.",
}
⟦ /bbh_lab_01_policy_atomization_spec ⟧
